Acute Pyelonephritis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Fever/rigors, Costovertebral angle tenderness, Nausea/vomiting, Leukocyte casts in urine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-003
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of rigors, high fever, and intractable vomiting with severe flank pain, requiring immediate assessment for sepsis and initiation of parenteral therapy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted high fevers, intractable vomiting preventing oral fluid intake, and severe unilateral back pain. Urgent after-hours evaluation was mandated to differentiate an upper urinary tract infection from a surgical abdominal emergency, and to initiate immediate antiemetic and antibiotic therapy to prevent imminent haemodynamic collapse before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right side is in agony, I can't stop shaking, and I've thrown up every sip of water since lunchtime."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced progressively over the past 48 hours, starting with severe dysuria, urinary frequency, and foul-smelling, cloudy urine. Twelve hours ago, the presentation sharply escalated with the sudden onset of high fever and associated violent rigors. The patient developed severe, constant right-sided flank pain. Nausea and recurrent profuse vomiting soon followed, rendering the patient unable to tolerate oral fluids or medications. She reports generalized fatigue, severe systemic malaise, and mild suprapubic discomfort.
Functional Status: Confined to bed due to severe systemic symptoms and orthostatic dizziness; requires assistance to ambulate to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending dehydration due to vomiting; adult daughter is present but anxious about the patient's rapidly deteriorating state.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 39.2C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, actively shivering, pale, and exhibiting signs of moderate clinical dehydration (dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes).
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Mild suprapubic tenderness. Back: Marked, severe costovertebral angle tenderness to percussion on the right side, causing the patient to flinch significantly. Cardiovascular: Tachycardia and mild hypotension consistent with early sepsis and volume depletion. Point-of-care urinalysis reveals gross haematuria, dense pyuria, and leukocyte esterase positivity, with expected leukocyte casts on formal microscopy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple emesis basins around the bed. Patient is heavily blanketed despite high core temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient attempted to take oral paracetamol but vomited the tablets intact. No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pyelonephritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of classic right lower quadrant peritonitis, negative Rovsing's sign, and presence of severe bacteriuria/flank pain.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the constant nature of the pain (non-colicky) and the prominent presence of high infectious fevers and rigors.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of lower pelvic peritonitis or vaginal discharge, and localized renal angle pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient presents with a classic septic triad of fever, flank pain, and vomiting secondary to upper urinary tract infection. The systemic inflammatory response is profound, evidenced by tachycardia, borderline hypotension, and rigors. Intractable vomiting completely precludes oral antibiotic and fluid administration, creating a high-risk scenario for rapid deterioration into septic shock. An urgent home visit was essential to administer parenteral antiemetics, initiate intramuscular empirical antibiotics, and assess for immediate hospital admission thresholds. Delaying care until routine in-hours GP availability would have placed the patient at imminent risk of irreversible septic complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg for nausea and IM Ceftriaxone 1g stat for immediate systemic antibacterial coverage. Administered SC fluids (hypodermoclysis) 500mL normal saline to initiate rehydration. Midstream urine collected.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration despite nausea, the patient is instructed to take one 10mL sip of oral rehydration solution every 10 minutes rather than large gulps. Successfully keeping these small volumes down will alleviate the dry mouth and dizziness, reinforcing the behavior of slow, paced fluid intake.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Hospital in the Home (HITH) referral considered for ongoing IV antibiotics if oral tolerance does not improve by morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Emesis bags provided for bedside.
Follow-up: GP review at 8:00 AM tomorrow to assess oral tolerance; if still vomiting, patient requires direct hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 is required if the patient becomes confused, experiences worsening dizziness upon standing (syncope), stops passing urine, or if vomiting persists despite the administered injection.

